and off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-kkh1an_results.md, TASK-260830-kkh1an_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-kkh1an_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-kkh1an --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n10|\n11|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n12|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n13|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n14|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n15|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n16|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n17|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n18|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n19|\n20|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, and traceability.go pointing at that trio. Cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above. If origin/main moves again before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate <this task>` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base.\n21|\n22|LANDED FACT YOU MUST NOT GET WRONG (found by the independent review of TASK-260830-1geqhj rev2, and it cost that leaf a whole round): `Observation.Winner.HasCheckpoint` / `Winner.Checkpoint` is a LEASE RECORD field, and lease records are immutable content-addressed blobs. `sessrepo.CreateLease` mints the epoch-1 lease with a NULL checkpoint, and only `CompareAndSwapLease` — a takeover, epoch >= 2 — mints a lease that carries one (Section 5.3: \"Null only for epoch-1 create; otherwise the validated materialized handoff base\"). No number of published checkpoints ever puts one on the epoch-1 lease. So any gate keyed on that field is VACUOUS for every session that never changed hands, which is the ordinary same-host story. The landed fact for \"the newest checkpoint\" is the chain fold: `sessstate.Reduce` -> `Projection.HasCheckpoint` / `Newest.ID`, derived from `checkpoint.created`, checkpointed `session.stopped` and `session.resumed` (Section 5.7 `newest_checkpoint_id`, Section 14.4). Key any window, admission or binding rule on that fold, and where a successor lease also carries a checkpoint, ASSERT the two agree rather than letting one silently win.\n23|\n24|INHERITED ADVISORIES from the independent review that accepted the predecessor leaf (RUN-260917-e644e0 accepted TASK-260830-1geqhj CR rev4 with no P1 and no P2, and recorded seven P3 items explicitly \"for the Story's follow-up leaves\" — that is you and TASK-260830-2056mm). None is a production defect on a path `Run` reaches today, but each is an unmeasured or under-specified seam in the code you are extending. Read them in TASK-260830-1geqhj_review-verdict-rev4.md and close the ones your scope touches, saying in your results which you closed and which you deliberately left to 2056mm:\n25|- - **P3-1 Unpinned member of the closure-source class: the journal-only restore path.** RV4-6 (`run.go`: read `observation.Winner.Checkpoint` instead of the fold's newest on exactly `Mode == ModeRestore && Winner.HasCheckpoint`, i.e. a restore with the journal required and NO required checkpoint) SURVIVED the whole committed suite twice. Witness `TestDX4_JournalOnlyRestoreClosureSource` (`logs/53-*`): the yolo→standard post-takeover world, restore with `MaterializationRequired` (journal from C1) and `CheckpointRequired=false` — pristine launches `standard`/`toStandard`, mapping empty; under the mutant it launches **`yolo` with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`** while `TestRunResto\n26|- - **P3-2 The pure core is not fail-closed on a missing closure.** `Decide` with `HasNewestCheckpoint=true`, no `CheckpointRequired` and empty `ProfileHeads` derives the SESSION HEAD: `TestR4HeadOnlyChangeAfterNewestNeverLaunches` (`logs/12`) — post-takeover world, C1 closure `standard`, a head-only `profile.changed → yolo` after the stop; `Run` launches `standard` in both modes (correct), `Decide` alone launches **`yolo` with the bypass mapping**. `Run` always supplies heads when the fold has a newest (an attested checkpoint carries 1..64 heads), so no `Run` path reaches this; the `Input` doc only says \"I/O-free callers supply them by hand\". Either refuse in `Decide` when the fold names a ne\n27|- - **P3-3 A remote-owned session whose newest checkpoint is absent from the LOCAL checkpoint store (or with no store bound) fails the run instead of reaching attach/takeover/park.** `TestR4RemoteOwnerNewestAbsentLocally` and `TestR4RemoteOwnerNoCkptStore` (`logs/12`): successor `(2,S)` on the remote host, chain folded locally, newest absent from the local `sessckpt` store → `Run` errors \"newest absent from the store\" / \"no checkpoint store\" with no decision, where `Decide` would return `attach_remote`. Fail-closed (no launch), but the after-restore step 4/5 outcomes become an error on a replica host with checkpoint lag; the closure is only needed on the launch path (local winner). Load the cl\n28|- - **P3-4 `EmitParked` does not bind the payload's `winning_lease_id` to the authoring lease.** `TestR4EmitParkedWinningLeaseMismatch` (`logs/13`): a `session.parked` event was appended under lease A with `payload.winning_lease_id = B`; RV4-13 (payload winner taken from `params.LeaseID`) SURVIVED ×2, so nothing distinguishes the decision's winner from the authoring lease. Through `Run` both come from `observation.Winner` (consistent); a direct caller can author a lying payload. Since `AuthorizeMutation` makes the authoring lease the winner, require `decision.WinningLeaseID == params.LeaseID` in `EmitParked` and pin it.\n29|- - **P3-5 Unpinned absence-vs-failure arm at `LoadCheckpoint`.** RV4-12 (any `sessckpt.Get` error laundered as absence) SURVIVED ×2. Witness `TestDX4_CorruptCheckpointBlobIsNotAbsence` (`logs/54-*`): a stored checkpoint blob corrupted in place (attestation failure, not ENOENT) — pristine: `Run` fails with the load error; under the mutant: \"newest absent from the store\". Both fail closed (no decision), the delta is the fact reported — the same class as rev3's P3-2. Add the corrupt-blob negative and a harness row.\n30|- - **P3-6 Reattach outcomes carry two terminal instance IDs.** `TestR4ReattachDescriptorNamesWhichInstance` (`logs/12`): on `reattach`, `Outcome.Binding.TerminalInstanceID` is the recorded child (1111) while `Decision.Descriptor.TerminalInstanceID` is the request's own/freshly minted instance (2222), built before the decision. `Decision.Descriptor` is documented as \"set on launch and reattach only\". Document that `Binding` is authoritative on reattach (or clear/rebuild the descriptor from the recorded child) so no caller targets a child that does not exist. Present since rev1; hygiene.\n31|- - **P3-7 Evidence archive hygiene.** `TASK-260830-1geqhj_rev4-evidence.tar.gz` is a tar of a shared scratch directory: its `README.md` is \"TASK-260830-24z2b3 producer evidence (rev4)\"; it carries `TASK-260830-24z2b3_results.md`/`_conformance-matrix.md`, a `TASK-260916-fsw7re` `logs-summary.txt`, `__pycache__/*.pyc` (3 files, listed in `MANIFEST.sha256`), `review-evidence-rev4.zip`, `mutants/run1|run2` (66 rows of a foreign task) and `mutants-run{1,2}.log`. The axpane evidence inside is genuine and matches my reruns (`mutants/pass1/` 54 raw logs with exits, `pass2-summary.log`, `axpane-test-v.log`, `ratio-check.log`, `cmd/cmd01..27`), but the archive breaks the task-scoped / no-`__pycache__` \n32|YOUR PREVIOUS CONSTRUCTION FAILED FOR A REASON THAT WAS NOT YOUR WORK — READ THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. Change Request revision 1 was built and its validation suite stopped at command 4 of 30 (`go test ./... -count=1 -v`) with exit 1, so the runtime recorded revision 1 as `changes_requested` even though NO reviewer ever saw it. The cause is a trunk defect that has since been fixed: `internal/resumesmoke/support_test.go` hard-coded a provider-call deadline of 2026-09-18T00:00:00.000Z, validated against a frozen 2026-09-17 fixture clock but enforced by `provhost` through `context.WithDeadline` on the REAL clock, so from 00:00Z the fake-provider child was killed before it answered and `go test ./...` went red on every tree. It was found by an independent reviewer on another task, reproduced on a clean trunk extract, and fixed as commit a12d1bd (BUG-260918-354b03, PR #53). Your Story branch base is e6fe5c5, which predates that fix, so the suite will fail the same way again until you move. Current origin/main is c3aae73 and carries BOTH the fix and STORY-260830-4qojoz (mesh RPC framing and negotiation, which also raised the configured suite from 27 to 30 commands). Run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-kkh1an` FIRST, then audit every path in `git diff --name-only e6fe5c5 c3aae73` that also appears in your `git status` — README.md, LOGBOOK.md and task-board.config.json all moved on trunk, so your edits must sit on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes, and the config must end byte-identical to the new base. Nothing about your scope changes: the Section 4.C lifecycle work and the seven inherited advisories stand exactly as written above. Everything you already wrote in the worktree is still there — read `git status --short` and `git diff` first, state in your results what you inherited, and verify it against this brief rather than redoing it.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3c3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b28c-5604-75c0-aae6-73b3ca1e890f"},"sequence":60,"recorded_at":1780531400000115,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d8b3c488-a0a9-443b-8e40-610c9c2591de","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"d8b3c488-a0a9-443b-8e40-610c9c2591de","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d8b3c488-a0a9-443b-8e40-610c9c2591de"},"call_id":"call_01a0b28c721777b390ba318417f07ab5","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-kkh1an/TASK-260830-kkh1an_rework-rev3.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES EVERY EARLIER PRECONDITION ON TASK-260830-kkh1an, including TASK-260830-kkh1an_producer.md (still the scope of record for WHAT the leaf owns, but its execution instructions are replaced by this one). Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-kkh1an (implement-terminal-instance-state-machine, the SECOND leaf of STORY-260830-ptxkqe; the final leaf TASK-260830-2056mm follows). Change Request revision 2 was reviewed by RUN-260918-4f5cc1 (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED with NO P1: two P2 production findings, two P2 evidence findings and seven P3. Read TASK-260830-kkh1an_review-verdict-rev2.md and TASK-260830-kkh1an_review-evidence-rev2.tar.gz first. The reviewer confirms on the exact tree that everything else you claim holds: the shipped harness reproduces 70/70 twice, the ratio re-derives to 72 of 72, the real-kill seam holds and hygiene is clean. Keep all of it. Note also that revision 1 carries NO review verdict — its construction died on a trunk defect since fixed — so there is nothing to look for there.\n3|F1 — make the same-key retry after `status` executable. Today `Bind` refuses unconditionally when it replays a receipt that has no completion, which means a receipt keeps its idempotency key forever and the operation-table recovery column \"status, then same-key retry\" plus create's \"idempotent across controller crash\" are unimplementable as written. Reconcile through the production status entry (or accept a fresh `StatusReport` the caller obtained) and do exactly what the row columns say: replay the recorded result when the target state is proven; continue the SAME operation from the proven source state when nothing happened (for create, absence proven by a successful status read means perform the effects under the same receipt and complete it); and keep refusing `status_first` only while the observation is genuinely unknown. Keep the existing SIGKILL evidence and extend it: crash at the receipt seam, then status, then a same-key retry completes with exactly one result and one instance and no second receipt — and a same-key retry WITHOUT a status read still refuses. Either fix the `StoreHooks` doc sentence or make it true at the engine. Ship a narrowing row per new arm.\n4|F2 — fence the stale incarnation under a remote winner. `ObserveFencing` never fences a stale incarnation whose winner is on another host, which is exactly the force-takeover case the pinned Section 13.3 step 15 exists for. Fix it WITHOUT a local tuple comparison: compose the landed fencing gate, do not fork it. Drive both remote members (older epoch, and same-epoch losing lease) from `active`, `parked` and `quiescing`, and keep the winning-token-under-foreign-host case as \"no transition\". Add narrowing rows.\n5|E1 — create-row negatives at the engine: control lease, expired lease and foreign lease authorization; stale generation; headless-from-`absent` without `headless_creation`; and create's second-effect committed timeout. Each pre-effect case must assert that NO receipt is bound. Re-key the harness rows `N-engine-auth-entry` and `N-engine-generation-entry` so that a mutant exempting `create` is killed — today they pass while create walks through.\n6|E2 — the per-effect recheck is pinned at one position only, and the reported session is never compared in any status test. Rotate the lease and the generation before the THIRD stop effect, and drift the reported session on both status lookup paths (plus each of the other five axes named as P3-5).\n7|P3 — P3-1 a pre-link store failure must restore the source state; P3-2 name the fencing sources or state the bound; P3-3 the deadline instant on both entries; P3-4 add `attach` and `none` to the kind negatives; P3-6 and P3-7 as you judge; and document `holder_host_id` as unbound. Then reword TRACEABILITY rows 70 and 71 and the \"recovers through status\" sentence in the results and LOGBOOK once F1 is in — those sentences are currently true only of the intent, not of the code.\n8|Procedure: your candidate sits on the predecessor checkpoint c61fc06 on a Story branch already refreshed onto c3aae73. Do not refresh unless origin/main actually moves; if it does, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-kkh1an` and audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`, keeping README/LOGBOOK edits additive on the NEW content. You are a MIDDLE leaf: do NOT touch internal/traceability — the final leaf TASK-260830-2056mm carries the registry bindings and the pin. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept. Re-run the full configured validation suite once (30 commands at trunk c3aae73 — read the count from the candidate config rather than assuming it; command 5 is the race gate with `-timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs). Update TASK-260830-kkh1an_results.md with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), update the conformance matrix and TASK-260830-kkh1an_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__), re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-kkh1an --role developer` so revision 3 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Report the ratio you MEASURE, not the one you planned.\n9|PARTIAL-ATTEMPT RECOVERY: this task has already lost runs to a provider idle timeout and to a trunk defect. If a previous attempt died mid-way, the Story worktree still carries its partial edits — start with `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-ptxkqe/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, state what you inherited, and verify it against this brief instead of assuming it is correct.\n10|Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 3.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
